tomorrow marks one year since the deadly  riots on our nation's capital the  january 6 insurrection brought renewed  attention to the issue of extremism as  we mark the one-year anniversary since  what do we now know about the rise of  extremism in our country and the dangers  it still presents here to share more  with us is oren siegel vice president of  the center of extremism at the  anti-defamation league oren great to  speak with you and welcome to quick take  when we look back on the sixth about 21  of attendees had overties to right-wing  extremist groups but about 80 percent  didn't have any overties to those  organizations  since then have things gotten better or  worse among the american electorate  things have not gotten better right the  the narratives and disinformation that  animated people and brought them to the  capitol on january 6th in the first  place have not gone away in fact what  we've seen is whether it's the extremist  groups who are involved uh in the  capital like the proud boys or the oath  keepers or the majority of people who  are not card-carrying members of any  extremist group  continue to show up locally  at school board hearings we continue to  see threats  against medical professionals so again  the grievances that undermine our  democratic institutions are playing out  more locally throughout 2021  so so what do we do then right i mean  you mentioned that this is playing out  at the individual level in really strong  ways i mean i've noticed that in my  reporting on covid with parents going  back and forth about issues coming up at  school board meetings  what do we do to to move past the kind  of division that january 6th really  represented  you know it's a really tough question  because i think accountability is  important and seeing the over 700  arrests for those who are  involved in the insurrection is  important  but we also need a shared understanding  of what happened on that day and frankly  we're just not on the same page in this  country there are people who consider  those who are arrested to be political  prisoners right we have cable news  channels who are promoting additional  conspiracy theories about who is behind  those activities and locally i think  disinformation is continuing to animate  people because of vaccine mandates  school board hearings etc so i think  what is required is to get on the same  page  and then secondarily we need to train  people to be critical thinkers in terms  of the information that they're bringing  in especially on social media  well i i want to talk  exactly about that media consumption  question because 2021 saw a huge dip in  engagement with the news across  mainstream media outlets like fox cnn  msnbc was all down 36 overall with  viewership how does news consumption or  in this case a lack of it over the past  year play into this conversation  you know it sort of depends on what  people consider news i think what  someone sees on their facebook feed they  believe is news or if they view it on  you know some fringe platform as long as  somebody has an opinion or is trying to  promote an agenda i think people view  that as news and not understanding the  difference between what is actual news  and what is propaganda is one of the  issues here this is the environment that  enables disinformation to proliferate  and it's the environment that frankly as  a requirement for extremists to be able  to make inroads the more that there's  confusion the more that there's division  the more that there's polarization the  people who win are the extremists and  frankly i think so much of our political  discussion has become an extremist  discussion  yeah so i'm curious who if you could  kind of snap your fingers right now and  change one thing that's currently  fueling disinformation extremism what  would that thing be would it be language  from politicians would it be big tech  social media where would you start  i mean listen  all of the above i think fundamentally  though if  certainly we need people to consistently  and clearly call out uh conspiracy  theories and hate but i also think  social media companies need to do a much  better job  identifying how those platforms are  being exploited  and making sure that they're transparent  about what they're doing to try to  prevent that right it's not unreasonable  for users of these platforms to expect  that they will do more to protect the  safety of their users but also to make  sure that those narratives that animate  people to action and in some cases  violence  are not being amplified on those  platforms it's no longer unreasonable to  have that expectation  and a lot of experts believe that 2022  is the year we're finally going to see  some big regulation on big tech to what  extent do you think that would really  make an impact  on disinformation and extremism is  regulation and big tech enough or will  people just you know make new social  media platforms or find other methods of  communication if uh you know social  platforms were to get more heavily  regulated  i mean  there clearly needs to be both right so  there needs to be efforts um from our  policy makers  to carefully reform not eliminate but  reform section 230 of a law that  basically allows social media platforms  unprecedented immunity from legal  accountability we also though need to  recognize that the more that some of the  big name social media companies do  uh to [ __ ] down on on extremism that  extremists will find multiple other  platforms to migrate to right this is an  ecosystem but again  it's not necessarily a bad thing is as  they're migrating to different platforms  they don't have as huge of a bull horn  to express their hatred but those  smaller more fringe and upcoming  platforms need to make sure that part of  their development is to address  extremists before they overtake those  platforms as well  and i hear you mentioning the importance  of accountability orin the capitol  police chief testified today that  threats have significantly increased  over the past year testifying before the  senate rules committee and saying there  were over 9500 threats this year which  is an average of 26 per day the highest  number they've ever had i mean what  grade would you give the response to  january 6 particularly the january 6th  panel do you think that the response to  the capital siege was enough  listen i think a lot remains to be seen  right they're still in the process i  think again over 700 people arrested for  their role in the insurrection  is progress that is a level of  accountability i think frankly though  that what people ought to expect  is that  this should have been a wake-up call  right this should have been an  opportunity what happened on january 6th  to reject extremism and the and the  narratives that animate it  instead we have in many ways seen a more  full embrace  of these messages that undermine our  democratic institutions that normalize  the messages and the tactics that  brought these extremists and others to  the capital in the first place this is  from our social media this is from  elected officials this is from our cable  news channels  i mean the fact that we don't have a  shared understanding of how bad what  happened on january 6th is  is not a good sign yeah  why do you think it wasn't a wake-up  call why wasn't it a bigger shift after  the sixth then after i think a lot of  people thought that biden entering the  white house might also lead to a shift  because violence and extremism is  politicized in this country  right  i think every incident that occurs  whether it's a mass shooting whether  it's protests whether it's an  insurrection  you know eventually becomes part of the  political football  and unfortunately the people that are  lose that lose are not only just all the  american people but frankly our  democracy  right i had no idea 20 years ago when i  started to look into extremism and hate  that that work would end up being an  effort to push back  against extremism who are fighting for  the soul of our democracy against the  soul of our democracy and so this is  just part of the regular conversation is  that what used to be  language and narratives that i would see  on some french platform are now  literally the public discussions that  we're having and so the normalization of  hate and extremism the inability to even  recognize it and call it out  i think is is an epidemic  so help me define this who are the  biggest groups involved here who are the  biggest groups represented on january  6th then who are they targeting now  so in in terms of the the 20 or so  percent  of those who've been arrested on january  6th who were part of you know more  organized or established extremist  groups or movements we're talking about  proud boys oath keepers  um followers of q anon  and they haven't gone away in fact so  much of the activity that we've seen  throughout 2021 from the proud boys has  focused on attending school board  hearings right in opposition to critical  race theory or vaccine or mask mandates  right they've localized their approach  they're trying to win hearts and minds  in the local space they don't need to go  to dc anymore  and others too q anon for example that  hasn't gone away that conspiracy theory  as absurd that it is continues to bring  people out in the streets like in dallas  where people thought jfk jr was going to  come back right so the online spaces in  which most much of this propaganda  spreads and the ideas are sort of  incubated  we are seeing the activity on the ground  throughout the country the front line  against tate is not just our nation's  capital right it's in every local  community that we're in  i think that's a great place to leave it  oren thank you so much for joining us  really appreciate you breaking that down  and explaining uh the rise of extremism  since january 6 and that led up to it as  well thank you so much that was oren  siegel vice president of the center on  extremism at the anti-defamation league  you
 

